{{also|chen|Chen|chēn|chén|chěn|chèn}}
==German==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|de|-ken}} {{qualifier|colloquial in northern Germany and Westphalia}}
* {{l|de|-gen}}

===Etymology===
Native to the Central German dialects, ''-chen'' has widely replaced the southern-based {{m|de|-lein}} in standard usage. Cognate with {{cog|nds-de|-ken}}, {{m|nds-de|-ke}} as well as {{cog|nl|-tje}} and {{m|nl|-ke}}. More at {{l|en|-kin}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/çən/|[çən]|[çn̩]|lang=de}}

===Suffix===
{{head|de|suffix|cat2=noun-forming suffixes|g=n|genitive|-chens|plural|-chen}}

# {{non-gloss definition|nowadays the most common German suffix to create a [[diminutive]] form; e.g. ''[[Hund]]'' ("dog") &rarr; ''Hünd'''chen''''' ("little dog")}}

====Usage notes====
* Words ending in the suffix ''-chen'' are always neuter, whatever gender the basic word.
* Generally, with few exceptions, diminutives with ''-chen'' have an [[umlaut]]ed stem vowel. Double vowels must be singled as the combinations ''ää'' and ''öö'' are not permitted in modern German orthography (e.g. {{m|de|Härchen}} from {{m|de|Haar}}, {{m|de|Bötchen}} from {{m|de|Boot}}).
* Words ending in ''-ch'' (and some others) use the double suffix {{m|de|-elchen}}, e.g. {{m|de|Strichelchen}} from {{m|de|Strich}}. However, the suffix {{m|de|-lein}} is quite common for these words in writing ({{m|de|Strichlein}}).
* Some words also have a (dated or colloquial) plural in ''-erchen'', e.g. {{m|de|Häuschen}}, {{m|de|Hühnchen}}, {{m|de|Kindchen}}, {{m|de|Lämmchen}}, {{m|de|Liedchen}}, {{m|de|Männchen}}.

